NautilusTrader — GNU Lesser General Public License v3.0 (LGPL-3.0-only)
  https://github.com/nautechsystems/nautilus_trader

  STATUS: active dependency. `crates/arvo-nautilus` is the only crate
  permitted to depend on it, and depends on `nautilus-backtest`,
  `nautilus-common`, `nautilus-core`, `nautilus-model` and
  `nautilus-trading` (all pinned `=0.63.0`), which in turn pull in
  `nautilus-analysis`, `-data`, `-execution`, `-portfolio`, `-risk` and
  `-system`. The whole backtest engine is now linked, not just the domain
  model. Everything below applies to real distributed artifacts.

  Arvo links NautilusTrader's Rust crates in-process (see
  arvo-desktop/.scratch/arvo-architecture/map.md). A statically linked binary is
  therefore a "Combined Work" under LGPL-3.0 section 4, and the obligations
  attach to distributed binaries — notably the Tauri installer — not to Arvo's
  own Apache-2.0 source.

  What that requires of anything we distribute:

    * Ship this notice and copies of the LGPL-3.0 and GPL-3.0 licence texts.
    * State prominently that NautilusTrader is used and is covered by LGPL-3.0.
    * Give recipients the means to relink against a modified NautilusTrader.
      Arvo satisfies this by publishing complete corresponding source and
      working build instructions: a recipient can substitute their own
      NautilusTrader and rebuild. (Rust has no stable Rust-to-Rust ABI, so the
      shared-library route in section 4(d)(0) is not practical here.)
    * Do not prohibit reverse engineering for debugging such modifications.

  Practical consequence: Arvo must remain buildable from published source. If
  Arvo ever ships a binary whose source is not published, this arrangement no
  longer discharges the obligation and the boundary must be revisited — moving
  NautilusTrader out of process would restore it, since arms-length IPC is not
  linking.

  This is a record of the mechanism, not legal advice. Confirm with counsel
  before any commercial distribution.

